00:04:56.522 --> 00:04:59.422
to the terminals SpaceX operates under contract.

00:05:01.345 --> 00:05:06.465
Reuse physics is the engineering angle today because a first stage only flies again if

00:05:06.465 --> 00:05:07.705
the energy budget closes.

00:05:08.325 --> 00:05:12.525
Each ascent burns roughly 400 tons of propellant to reach staging velocity.

00:05:13.085 --> 00:05:17.485
The rocket equation says the velocity gain depends on how much of the vehicle is spent

00:05:17.485 --> 00:05:18.065
as exhaust.

00:05:18.065 --> 00:05:23.365
Most of that ascent load exists to accelerate the rest of the ascent load, plus the upper

00:05:23.365 --> 00:05:23.845
stage.

00:05:24.345 --> 00:05:29.705
The return dumps the gained energy in 3 burns, boost back to reverse, entry to survive

00:05:29.705 --> 00:05:32.345
Mach 8, and landing over the drone ship.

00:05:32.845 --> 00:05:38.505
A back-of-envelope check on that Mach number, sea level speed of sound is about 340 meters

00:05:38.505 --> 00:05:42.685
per second, so it is on the order of 2.7 kilometers per second.

00:05:42.685 --> 00:05:42.805
speed.

00:05:43.265 --> 00:05:48.725
Textbook orbital speed is about 7.8 kilometers per second, which is why the first stage is

00:05:48.725 --> 00:05:49.825
not the orbital vehicle.

00:05:50.285 --> 00:05:53.165
It never has to kill full orbital energy on the way home.

00:05:53.765 --> 00:05:55.985
It still has to kill a hypersonic chunk of it.

00:05:56.485 --> 00:06:01.525
Kinetic energy scales with the square of speed, so even a fraction of orbital velocity is not

00:06:01.525 --> 00:06:02.285
a gentle stop.

00:06:02.845 --> 00:06:06.925
The entry burn exists to cut that speed before the airframe has to eat it as heat.

00:06:06.925 --> 00:06:12.045
speed. The structure sees peak dynamic pressure twice per mission, plus the thermal pulse

00:06:12.045 --> 00:06:17.585
of that hypersonic return. Dynamic pressure is the punch of air at speed, density times

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:22.425
velocity squared over 2. The stage gets one punch going up through the thick atmosphere

00:06:22.785 --> 00:06:28.445
and another coming back in. Thermal protection and the tank walls have to like both. Because

00:06:28.445 --> 00:06:33.565
the vehicle was size for a single use plus margin, extra propellant for recovery is only

00:06:33.565 --> 00:06:35.485
only about 30% of the Ascent total.

00:06:36.125 --> 00:06:40.905
Recovery burns right on leftover propellant after staging, so the vehicle is not lifting

00:06:40.905 --> 00:06:41.985
a second full stack.

00:06:42.565 --> 00:06:46.905
The stage spends that margin while still carrying fluid, then re-enters lighter.

00:06:47.485 --> 00:06:52.085
That is why the recovery allocation can stay a minority of the Ascent load instead of doubling

00:06:52.085 --> 00:06:52.645
the vehicle.

00:06:53.245 --> 00:06:57.985
Over many cycles, cumulative propellant costs per flight falls by a factor of roughly

00:06:57.985 --> 00:07:01.065
30 compared with building a new stage each time.

00:07:01.065 --> 00:07:06.685
The magic wand number is the raw inputs, kerosene, liquid oxygen, and tank metal.

00:07:07.285 --> 00:07:12.505
The idiot index, how many times the finished booster costs over those raw inputs, stays

00:07:12.505 --> 00:07:18.125
high because turbo pumps and avionics dwarf the commodity bill. A turbo pump is not priced

00:07:18.125 --> 00:07:23.085
like aluminum. Avionics and turbo pumps do not get cheaper just because the stage flies

00:07:23.085 --> 00:07:28.945
again. They get amortized. The floor is the sum of propellant, a small slice of those components

00:07:28.945 --> 00:07:34.865
life, and however many technician hours the pad still demands. If inspection, refurbishment,

00:07:35.125 --> 00:07:40.825
and launch site overhead drop below 10% of original build cost, per-flight economics approach

00:07:40.825 --> 00:07:46.785
that raw material and avionics floor. If the hours come down, the idiot index of a flown stage

00:07:46.785 --> 00:07:52.325
collapses toward the magic wand number. Leave the hours high and it is an expendable rocket that

00:07:52.325 --> 00:07:58.445
happens to land. Ground operations now set the next limit, not the physics of the burns. Orbital

00:07:58.445 --> 00:08:02.985
Little class first stages stay harder to reuse than suborbital hoppers for the same energy

00:08:02.985 --> 00:08:03.365
reason.

00:08:03.905 --> 00:08:08.405
A student scale hopper like Colibri only needs a low-speed vertical landing at roughly

00:08:08.405 --> 00:08:12.365
1.2 kN of thrust and 100 kG of mass.

00:08:12.905 --> 00:08:18.325
It never sees hypersonic heating, multi-engine throttling after 100-plus-kilometer trajectory,

00:08:18.825 --> 00:08:21.965
or a boost back while still carrying residual upper-stage mass.

00:08:22.565 --> 00:08:27.645
Those loads scale with the square velocity and the cube of linear dimensions, so a clean

00:08:27.645 --> 00:08:30.725
Green hopper landing does not become operational orbital reuse.

00:08:31.325 --> 00:08:36.285
A back-of-envelope figure on the Q-blaw, stretch a vehicle three times longer in every

00:08:36.285 --> 00:08:41.345
dimension, and the mass to keep intact is on the order of 27 times, not 3.

00:08:41.965 --> 00:08:46.865
Velocity squared on the heat and arrow-side stacks on top of that, which is the gap between

00:08:46.865 --> 00:08:48.485
a hopper and an orbital booster.

00:08:49.065 --> 00:08:54.085
A hopper at that mass never pays the heat tax, so its idiot index is mostly structure

00:08:54.085 --> 00:08:59.765
and avionics. Not thermal protection and engine out throttle profiles. None of that is a reason

00:08:59.765 --> 00:09:05.205
reuse fails. It is why the recovery propellant margin and a conservative tank set had to be in

00:09:05.205 --> 00:09:11.325
the original design. Not bolted on later. Falcon 9 absorbs the orbital loads by carrying extra

00:09:11.325 --> 00:09:16.705
propellant margin and flying the same structural tank set through repeated thermal and pressure cycles.

00:09:17.285 --> 00:09:22.385
Each extra flight checks that the original factor of safety assumption still hold after another

00:09:22.385 --> 00:09:28.265
full envelope exposure. Super heavy, starships first stage, lives under that same accounting,

00:09:28.725 --> 00:09:33.405
and comparable flight counts would make rapid reuse the dominant advantage over expendable

00:09:33.405 --> 00:09:38.965
architectures. The remaining question is process, whether inspection hours and fatigue allow

00:09:38.965 --> 00:09:44.105
that floor to be reached, or whether tear down rules get there first. On the public tape,

00:09:44.565 --> 00:09:48.125
SPCX slipped roughly half a percent versus the prior close.

